+| Keep reading | 8 | 9 | The chapter-10 close (`chapter-10.md:289–299`, Lira pointing “Behind you”) and chapter-11 opening convert one cliff into a new tactical problem. The only lull is the extended recovery/accounting run before the well; the adult lens is more likely to value all of its thematic accumulation. | Medium / High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9 | 9 | `chapter-09.md:133–205` turns animal behavior, water, discharge, and a tracker’s “cut” into a novel plan; `chapter-14.md:177–221` makes bad air and a thorn faggot a second, wholly different progression problem. | Medium / High |
+| Clarity / flow | 8 | 9 | `chapter-08.md:217–283` establishes the sill, lip, pools, bar, Brom's stone, and Lira's run before the fight. The geography pays off cleanly, though a younger reader must hold near/far banks, head/tail, and three groups of hounds in mind through `chapter-10.md:69–155`. | Medium / High |
+| Character attachment | 9 | 9 | `chapter-13.md:177–235` lets Lira state the actual loss and lets Cael understand why both reassurance and a replacement score would be wrong. Comparable attachment beats land for Karis at `chapter-11.md:31–55`, Brom at `chapter-13.md:291–341`, and Seln at `chapter-13.md:247–275`. | Medium / High |
