+# Editorial review — Movement 002, chapters 8–14
+
+## Review status and coverage
+
+This is a fresh-context simulated editorial read by the review seat, with canon and formula evidence supplied. It is not the author's self-review, not a blind cold read, and not human audience testing.
+
+Coverage is the complete supplied movement: seven chapters, chapter 8 through chapter 14, 36,806 prose words. I read them in order. The movement is complete on its own stated range, days 51–69 past the Line.
+
+**Verdict: pass for continuity, story, Reader Standard, protected wording, and source-distance. Formula alignment is mixed rather than failed:** the three primary working-range measures pass, while reading ease and sentence-width remain materially lighter/narrower than the selected targets. No continuity repair is required.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest catches immediately at `chapter-08.md:3`, when the mule's pace wakes Cael before he understands why, and tightens when Pike strikes Teague's hold in public. The strongest sustained sequence is the ford: the noon reconnaissance makes every later choice legible, the loft turns observations into a dangerous hypothesis, and the fight pays off the terrain, the one-declaration rule, each companion's practiced refusal, and the stillhounds' capacity to learn. The action has genuine consequence without becoming cruel spectacle.
+
+Interest slips only a little after the ford, during the long recovery-and-accounting stretch in chapters 12–13. That material is worthwhile—the injuries change how the group works, and each companion gets a distinct question—but a thirteen-year-old reader may feel the forward plot pause before the well. The well restores immediate physical pressure and gives Cael an excellent, quiet development beat.
+
+Lira matters most to me in this movement. Her discovery that she is fast without Wind is exhilarating; her later question—`chapter-13.md:185`, “So what am I becoming, exactly, if nobody's measuring?”—turns the injury and the board's silence into a personal loss that Cael cannot solve for her. Brom, Karis, and Seln each receive a comparably specific turn rather than functioning as helpers.
+
+I expect the blank tack and the coming hold slip at `chapter-14.md:455–463` to pull the crew into the next road problem while the quiet-ground phrase waits in Karis's box. Yes, I would continue.
+
+## Reader lenses
+
